@meta
id: 10-09
title: What double-click gets wrong
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-word, obj-quote, inner-vs-around
par: 52
minutes: 6
vscode: double-click, then drag to fix what the double-click missed
open: order.mailer.ts

@brief
Double-click is the one selection gesture that is genuinely good in a
mouse editor, and it is worth being honest about where it stops.

Double-click on `customerId` in `order.customerId` and you get
`customerId`. That is `iw`, and it is right. Double-click inside
`'order-shipped'` and you get `order` — half a string, because the dash
ends the word. Double-click on `order.customer.email` and you get one
segment of three. In both of those cases you go back to dragging, and
dragging is how a stray quote ends up inside your string literal.

Vim's answer is that you say which object you mean rather than hoping one
gesture guesses it.

The notification payload has been flattened upstream. `payload.order` is
gone: the customer now hangs off the payload directly, and so does the
order id. While you are in there, the event name has changed to
`order-dispatched`.

Three edits. The file is nine lines. Do not retype any line in full.

@keys
| `iw` `aw` | one word — what double-click gives you |
| `d2aw` | two words and their punctuation, so `order.` goes in one go |
| `ci'` | the whole string, dashes included |
| `ciW` | whitespace to whitespace, semicolon and all |

@hints
Removing `order.` from a chain is not a word deletion. It is a word, and
then the dot after it.
--
Objects take counts exactly like motions do. `d2aw` from the start of
`order` deletes `order` and the `.` that follows it.
--
`3fo` lands on the `o` of `order` on the first line. Then `d2aw`. The
string is `ci'` from the start of its line, and the last chain is the same
`3fo` and `d2aw` again.

@solution
    2j 3fo d2aw
    j 0 ci' order-dispatched <Esc>
    3j 0 3fo d2aw
    :wq<Enter>

Forty-five keystrokes for three edits in three different shapes of text,
and not one of them needed a selection.

The lesson underneath it is about naming. A mouse has one gesture and it
has to guess which thing you meant. Vim makes you say it: `iw` for the
identifier, `iW` for the whole dotted chain, `i'` for the string, and the
count in `2aw` for exactly two of them. The cost is that you have to know
the names. The return is that you are never fighting a selection that is
one character off.

Worth trying by hand afterwards: put the cursor on `customer` in the first
line and press `ciW`. You will get the whole of `payload.customer.email;`
including the semicolon, because `W` runs to the next space. That is
sometimes exactly right and sometimes a trap, and the only way to know
which is to have felt both.

@start order.mailer.ts
export class OrderMailer {
  public async sendShipped(payload: ShippedPayload) {
    const recipient = payload.order.customer.email;
    const template = 'order-shipped';

    return this.emailService.send(recipient, template, {
      orderId: payload.order.id,
    });
  }
}

@expected order.mailer.ts
export class OrderMailer {
  public async sendShipped(payload: ShippedPayload) {
    const recipient = payload.customer.email;
    const template = 'order-dispatched';

    return this.emailService.send(recipient, template, {
      orderId: payload.id,
    });
  }
}
